            {
              "type": "code_body",
              "index": 0,
              "bbox": [
                0.124,
                0.403,
                0.811,
                0.508
              ],
              "content": "Algorithm to find the divisors of n\n1. Start with an empty list-of-divisors.\n2. For each number j in the sequence 1,2,3,...,n\n– if j divides n, add j to the list-of-divisors."
            }
          ],
          "sub_type": "code",
          "guess_lang": "txt"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.072,
            0.148,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "40"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.071,
            0.494,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.111,
            0.66,
            0.133
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11.2.1 Computing the Divisors of a Number"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.142,
            0.587,
            0.163
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Suppose we want to compute gcd(375, 825)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.162,
            0.883,
            0.239
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How do we find the divisors of 375? The divisors of a number are just the factors of that number. Clearly any divisor, or factor, must lie between 1 and 375. The simplest way to find the divisors is to check each number between 1 and 375 and see if it divides 375."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.239,
            0.883,
            0.353
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We also have to keep track of the divisors of 375 so that we can compare them with the divisors of 825. We can maintain each of these in a sequence, or a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "list",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". How do we create the list of divisors of a number? When we start, we do not know any divisors, so the list of divisors is initially empty. Every time we find a new divisor, we add it at the end of the list."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.353,
            0.883,
            0.393
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "divisors of any number n."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.52,
            0.882,
            0.598
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Notice how we use names list-of-divisors and j to identify the values that we keep track of in this algorithm. If we do not use j to identify the number we are checking next as a possible divisor, it would be clumsy to describe what to do each time we find a divisor."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.598,
            0.882,
            0.676
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Running through an algorithm step by step is also called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "executing",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " the algorithm. Let us execute this algorithm for a small number, say 18. We use square brackets to denote a list of values — for instance, [1, 4, 9, 16]."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.68,
            0.837,
            0.701
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us see how this algorithm works for a small number, say 18."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.706,
            0.601,
            0.727
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Initially list-of-divisors is empty [ ]."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.732,
            0.731,
            0.753
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "• 1 divides 18, so we update list-of-divisors to [1]."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.758,
            0.755,
            0.779
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "• 2 divides 18, so we update list-of-divisors to [1, 2]."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.784,
            0.778,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "• 3 divides 18, so we update list-of-divisors to [1, 2, 3]."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.811,
            0.881,
            0.85
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4 does not divide 18, so we leave list-of-divisors unchanged as [1, 2, 3]."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.856,
            0.882,
            0.896
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5 does not divide 18, so we leave list-of-divisors unchanged as [1, 2, 3]."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
